    no clock of its own — [38;5;153mnow[39m is passed in, which is what makes the notification rules testable without waiting for time
    to pass.
  - [1m[38;5;153msrc/store/[0m — the data-access layer. A [38;5;153mTaskStore[39m interface ([38;5;153mlist[39m, [38;5;153mget[39m, [38;5;153mcreate[39m, [38;5;153mupdate[39m, [38;5;153mremove[39m) with a
    [38;5;153mLocalStorageTaskStore[39m implementation behind it. This is the single mutation path, and it emits a change event on
    every write. Two things depend on that: the UI re-renders, and the notification scheduler re-evaluates. This module
    is the whole "structured to grow" bet — swapping in an [38;5;153mApiTaskStore[39m later is what makes multi-user a swap rather
    than a rewrite.
  - [1m[38;5;153msrc/ui/[0m — renders a task list from state and calls store methods. Never reads [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, never computes due-soon
    itself; it asks the model.
  - [1m[38;5;153msrc/notify/[0m [3m(sub-project 2)[0m — subscribes to store changes, computes which tasks are due-soon or overdue, and
    dispatches through a [38;5;153mNotifier[39m interface. Two implementations: an in-app inbox and the service worker. It reads
    through the store like everything else.

  A [38;5;153mTask[39m carries: [38;5;153mid[39m, [38;5;153mtitle[39m, [38;5;153mdueDate[39m (optional), [38;5;153mdone[39m, [38;5;153mcreatedAt[39m, and [38;5;153mownerId[39m. That last one is hardcoded to a single
  local user for now — it costs one field today and is painful to add to stored records later.

  [1mTooling question, since this is the moment it's cheapest.[0m Setting these up before any code exists is far cheaper than
  retrofitting, and whatever you pick becomes a constraint every later task inherits.
